  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Sida Nigeria Fair Finance 24 27 CISLAC</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Nigeria is rich in natural resources, but the benefits of these are not visibly reaching poor people. The lack of accountability and transparency mechanisms are mostly prevalent within the extractive industries. Furthermore, country specific issues include, amongothers, a regressive tax system, unequal wealth distribution, non-adherence to responsible working conditions (labour laws). Taxingthe digital economy, or more specifically large companies such as Google or Amazon is difficult for the revenue authority in Nigeria, because of the absence of a physical presence in the country.88 This reduces public investments in critical sectors such as education, health, agriculture, and social security. Major stakeholders include financial institutions both public and private.
Fair Finance Nigeria intends to: (1) Reduce leakages and illicit financial flows aided by FIs in the oil and gas industry through production of research and policy papers (2) Building on the research and policy papers engage with companies and key investors in the extractives sector to press them into developing stronger policies and demanding policy compliance from companies they invest in. (3) Through research and policy papers, expose digital channels through which financial transactions are lost, in light of national tax policyon digital economy. (4) Building on research and policy papers, engage with Nigerian regulatory bodies (5) Conduct training for investigative journalists and CSOs on Fair Finance and organize capacity building for communities to make their voices heard, target and hold companies and policymakers to account.
Case studies are planned on extractive industries and the digital economy. Policy assessments will be carried out, covering banks who are active both in Ghana and Nigeria.

Fair Finance Nigeria consists of: Oxfam in Nigeria, Connected Development Initiative (CODE), BudgIT, Society for Women in Taxation-SWIT, Civil Society Legislative Advocacy Centre (CISLAC), Policy Alert, Paradigm Leadership Support Initiative (PLSI), Social Development Integrated Centre (Social Action), Support for Training and Entrepreneurial Program (STEP), and the Nigeria Labour Congress.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org role="4" type="80">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CISLAC</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Civil Society Legislative Advocacy Centre (CISLAC) was set up in 2005 to strengthen the link between civil society and the Nigerian legislature. It was officially registered in 2006. Its priority areas are anti-corruption, fiscal responsibility, public procurement, the Nigerian Extractives Industries Transparency Initiative (NEITI) Act and budget tracking. Its main intervention areas are lobby, advocacy, research and capacity building of civil society organisations, legislators and media. CISLAC has been very instrumental in passing the NEITI Act in 2007 and monitoring and assisting the Nigerian government in its implementation.</narrative>
  </participating-org>
